# Example candor effect policy.  Run with:  cargo candor policy examples/candor-policy
# (or drop it at .candor/policy and run `cargo candor policy`).
#
# Each rule constrains the effects a function may *transitively* perform — so it catches a layer that
# reaches an effect through a helper, not just a direct call. That's the architectural invariant an
# AI agent can't see from a local edit: it edits one function; candor sees the whole effect graph.
#
# Scope = a substring of the fully-qualified function path (module / type / fn); omit it for crate-wide.
#
#   deny <Effects...> [scope]    forbid those effects in scope   (effects: Net Fs Db Exec Env Clock
#                                                                  Ipc Log Rand Clipboard Unknown)
#   pure [scope]                 forbid ALL effects in scope (must be side-effect-free)
#   allow Net [in <scope>] <host>...   in scope, Net may reach ONLY these hosts — any other endpoint
#                                      (or one candor can't see) is a violation (AS-EFF-008). An
#                                      allowlist a model can't self-check: the host is a literal buried
#                                      in a transitive, possibly cross-crate, callee.
#   forbid <A> -> <B>            a function in scope A must not (transitively) call into scope B — the
#                                dependency-direction rule (AS-EFF-009). "domain must not reach infra."
#
# `#` comments and blank lines are ignored. Enforce a whole workspace in one command with
# `cargo candor policy` (it snapshots every crate, then enforces with siblings loaded).

# The domain / business-logic layer must not reach the network, a database, or the filesystem.
deny Net Db Fs   domain

# Parsing and validation must be pure — no I/O, clock, or randomness.
pure             parse

# Nothing in this crate should ever spawn a subprocess.
deny Exec

# The billing module may reach the network, but ONLY Stripe — a call that ends up hitting any other
# host (even through a deep or cross-crate helper) is flagged. This is the supply-chain boundary: a
# model editing billing can't see which endpoint a transitive callee actually connects to.
allow Net in billing  api.stripe.com  hooks.stripe.com

# Layering: the domain layer must not depend on infrastructure — not even through a chain of helpers.
forbid domain -> infra
